  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tunda Finance - Sustainable Growth in the Avocado Oil Sector in Tanzania</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A key challenge in the Tanzanian avocado market is that the maximum productive potential of an avocado tree will not yield more than 65 percent Grade A exportable fruit. Consequently, about 35 percent of the fruit is always rejected, classified as lower grade, and creates zero income for farmers. To valorise the rejected - mostly Hass - avocado&#x2019;s, Tazanian project partner Avomeru buys lower grade avocados and processes them into oil. However, due to systemic constraints such as lack of access to finance (to purchase fertiliser, new seedlings, irrigation models etc.) and limited organic soil inputs resulting in the depletion of soil health, more is required to help smallholders improve productivity and therefore income. Commercial partners in this Impact Cluster project address these constraints, because they have a long term interest in the valorisation of the lower grade avocado production: &#x2022;	Agricultural finance: Emata will credit score 1500 farmers and offer access to loans earning a revenue on each loan.  &#x2022;	Fertiliser sales: Marula Agribusiness will produce 2,000 tons of organic (mixed) fertiliser from waste per harvest to be sold to the farmers to increase productivity, through circular waste-to-fertiliser innovations using black solder flies.  &#x2022;	Production volume increase: The increase in farmers yields will also increase the input volume for Avomeru allowing them to scale oil production with contracted farmers.  &#x2022;	Organic certification: Avomeru will increase the sales price of avocado oil with organic certification.</narrative>
  </description>
